---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { SITE, withBase } from "../lib/site";

const effectiveDate = "September 2, 2026";
const sections = [
  ["overview", "Overview"],
  ["information", "Information and data"],
  ["services", "Third-party services"],
  ["advertising", "Advertising and consent"],
  ["retention", "Retention and controls"],
  ["rights", "Your privacy rights"],
  ["children", "Children"],
  ["security", "Security and transfers"],
  ["changes", "Changes and contact"],
];
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="How NutCracker Tools processes local app data, Firebase diagnostics and analytics, advertising consent, notifications, and website preferences."
  page="privacy"
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero__inner shell">
      <span class="eyebrow">Privacy policy</span>
      <h1>Specific about the data flow.</h1>
      <p>This policy covers the NutCracker Tools Android app and this official website. It separates information kept on your device from information processed by network services.</p>
      <div class="page-hero__meta">
        <span class="chip chip--warm">Effective · {effectiveDate}</span>
        <span class="chip">Android · {SITE.packageName}</span>
        <span class="chip chip--teal">No app account required</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section section--flush-top">
    <div class="legal-layout shell">
      <nav class="legal-nav aside-card" aria-label="Privacy policy contents">
        <strong>On this page</strong>
        <ul>{sections.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a></li>)}</ul>
      </nav>

      <article class="legal-content">
        <div class="legal-intro">
          <p><strong>Plain-language summary:</strong> NutCracker Tools has no user-account system. App preferences and information used by local features are handled primarily on your device. Connected features may share request and technical data with the service providers listed below. The Android app uses Google Firebase for analytics, diagnostics, performance monitoring, configuration, and optional messaging, and Google Mobile Ads when its consent flow permits ad requests. This website stores preferences locally and does not use website analytics or advertising trackers.</p>
        </div>

        <section class="legal-section" id="overview">
          <h2>1. Overview and scope</h2>
          <p>{SITE.operator} (“we,” “us,” or “our”) provides NutCracker Tools, Android package <strong>{SITE.packageName}</strong>, and this companion website. This policy explains what information is processed, why it is processed, where it goes, how long it may remain, and the controls available to you.</p>
          <p>The current Android app does not create or maintain login accounts, profiles, passwords, purchases, or subscriptions. We did not find first-party collection of precise location, contacts, camera, microphone, biometric data, phone numbers, or call information.</p>
          <div class="legal-callout">
            <strong>Important distinction</strong>
            <p>“Stored locally” means data remains in the app’s private storage or a browser on your device unless Android, your browser, or a listed network provider processes related technical information. Normal network connections reveal metadata such as an IP address to the receiving service.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section" id="information">
          <h2>2. Information and data we process</h2>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="legal-table">
              <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Examples</th><th>Where it is handled</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>App preferences</td><td>Theme, onboarding state, access method, target package, notification choices, ad-frequency counters</td><td>Primarily on your device</td><td>Remember settings and operate the app</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Local content state</td><td>Saved items, dashboard cache, local search index, and content markers</td><td>On your device</td><td>Faster browsing and continuity</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Activity records</td><td>Recent activity, results, timestamps, and recovery data</td><td>On your device</td><td>History, restoration, rollback, and recovery</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Product analytics</td><td>Screen views, onboarding progress, access mode, theme, setup phases, general feature usage, and bounded operation results and durations</td><td>Google Firebase Analytics</td><td>Understand use and improve product journeys</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Diagnostics and performance</td><td>Crash stack traces, app/device environment, installation identifiers, app version, performance traces and network timing</td><td>Firebase Crashlytics and Performance Monitoring</td><td>Find crashes, reliability problems, and slow operations</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Advertising data</td><td>Consent choices, ad interactions, device/advertising identifiers or Android ad signals where allowed</td><td>Google Mobile Ads, UMP, and advertising partners disclosed in the consent form</td><td>Request, deliver, measure, and limit ads</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Optional messaging</td><td>Firebase installation/registration identifiers and fixed topic subscriptions</td><td>Firebase Cloud Messaging after opt-in</td><td>Announcements, content updates, and important notices</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Website preferences</td><td>Theme, setup-assistant choices, and local dashboard activity notes</td><td>Your browser’s local storage</td><td>Remember this website’s state</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h3>Custom analytics limits</h3>
          <p>Our custom analytics events are designed not to include content you provide, free-form text, credentials, authentication tokens, or device identifiers. Firebase SDKs may still generate installation identifiers and standard app, device, network, and technical metadata under Google’s own documentation and settings. The current app does not provide an Analytics opt-out.</p>

          <h3>Website hosting</h3>
          <p>This website is static and does not install website analytics, ad pixels, account cookies, or a form backend. GitHub Pages and its infrastructure may receive request information such as IP address, browser/device details, requested URL, and timestamps for hosting, security, abuse prevention, and operations. See the <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub General Privacy Statement</a>.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section" id="services">
          <h2>3. Third-party services</h2>
          <p>Connected features may use:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Google Firebase:</strong> Analytics, Crashlytics, Performance Monitoring, Remote Config, Firebase Installations, and optional Cloud Messaging.</li>
            <li><strong>Google advertising services:</strong> AdMob/Google Mobile Ads, User Messaging Platform, Play Install Referrer, Android Ad Services, and consent-form partners.</li>
            <li><strong>Content delivery services:</strong> External APIs and content-delivery networks used when you load or download content.</li>
            <li><strong>Website hosting:</strong> GitHub Pages.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Data used only by local features remains on your device. When you use a connected feature, the relevant provider may receive the request data needed for that feature and standard technical information such as your IP address, language, device or browser details, and timestamps.</p>
          <p>Learn more from <a href="https://firebase.google.com/support/privacy">Firebase Privacy and Security</a> and the <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google Privacy Policy</a>.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section" id="advertising">
          <h2>4. Advertising and consent</h2>
          <p>NutCracker Tools supports dashboard banner ads, interstitial ads, and app-open ads. The app asks Google’s User Messaging Platform (UMP) to update consent information and only initializes the Mobile Ads SDK when UMP reports that ad requests may be made.</p>
          <p>Depending on your location, consent choices, Google’s current configuration, and available ad inventory, Google may serve personalized, non-personalized, limited, or technical-delivery ads. Even non-personalized ads can use identifiers or local storage for frequency capping, fraud prevention, and aggregated reporting. The active consent form identifies the participating advertising vendors and purposes.</p>
          <p>Firebase Analytics advertising-ID collection is explicitly disabled in the app, and default ad-personalization signals are false. Those settings do not mean that the separate Google Mobile Ads SDK processes no device or advertising data.</p>
          <p>Where UMP provides a privacy-options entry point, you can revisit ad choices from NutCracker Tools Settings. See Google’s <a href="https://developers.google.com/admob/android/privacy">UMP privacy guidance</a> and <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/ads">advertising technologies information</a>.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section" id="retention">
          <h2>5. Retention, deletion, and controls</h2>
          <h3>On your device</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Visible install history is capped at 50 entries and can be cleared in the app.</li>
            <li>Saved items, preferences, local access grants, caches, and operational records remain until changed, revoked, or the app’s storage is cleared.</li>
            <li>Disabling optional announcements unregisters Cloud Messaging and clears the locally stored registration identifier.</li>
            <li>Clearing app storage or uninstalling removes the app’s private local data. Some access choices can also be managed through Android settings.</li>
            <li>This website’s dashboard data can be erased from the web dashboard Privacy section or through browser site-data controls.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Clearing visible history does not currently remove every separate activity marker. The Android app does not yet provide a single in-app “erase all data” command.</p>

          <h3>Service-provider retention</h3>
          <p>Google applies service-specific retention rules to Firebase, Analytics, advertising, consent, and messaging data. Some retention settings are controlled in the project consoles and are not defined in the app source. Current examples and timeframes are published in <a href="https://firebase.google.com/support/privacy">Firebase’s privacy documentation</a>. GitHub applies its own retention rules to hosting and security data.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section" id="rights">
          <h2>6. Your choices and privacy rights</h2>
          <p>Depending on where you live, you may have rights to request access, correction, deletion, restriction, objection, portability, or information about certain processing, and to withdraw consent where processing relies on consent. You may also have the right to complain to a local data-protection authority.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Use Android settings to revoke app permissions.</li>
            <li>Use NutCracker Tools Settings to change notification topics and available Google ad-privacy choices.</li>
            <li>Clear history in the app, clear app storage, or uninstall the app to remove local private data as described above.</li>
            <li>Use the web dashboard’s erase control or clear this site’s data in your browser.</li>
            <li>Use Google’s privacy controls for data handled by Google services.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Because the app has no account system and much of its data is local or associated with pseudonymous service identifiers, we may not be able to identify a particular record from your name alone.</p>
          <div class="legal-callout">
            <strong>Privacy requests</strong>
            <p>Start through our <a href={withBase("support/")}>Support page</a>. If using the public issue tracker, do not post personal information, device identifiers, or credentials. Ask for a private follow-up channel.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section" id="children">
          <h2>7. Children’s privacy</h2>
          <p>NutCracker Tools is not directed to children under 13, and we do not knowingly seek personal information from children. If the law where you live sets a higher minimum age for consenting to data processing, use the app only with authorization from a parent or legal guardian.</p>
          <p>The current app does not include an age gate or a child-directed advertising flag. A parent or guardian who believes a child has provided information through a listed service should contact us and the relevant service provider. We will review this section and the advertising configuration before intentionally offering the app to a child-directed audience.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section" id="security">
          <h2>8. Security and international processing</h2>
          <p>We use platform controls such as Android private storage, system access controls, bounded analytics fields, and HTTPS for many service connections. No method of storage or transmission is completely secure.</p>
          <p>Some content downloads currently use allow-listed cleartext HTTP endpoints. This can expose requested paths and transferred content to network intermediaries. Avoid untrusted networks and keep the app updated while we work to reduce that exposure.</p>
          <p>Google, GitHub, and content providers may process information in countries other than your own, subject to their terms, safeguards, and applicable law. Firebase describes global processing locations and security practices in its privacy documentation.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="legal-section" id="changes">
          <h2>9. Changes and contact</h2>
          <p>We may update this policy when the app, website, service providers, catalog hosts, legal requirements, or privacy controls change. We will revise the effective date and, when appropriate, provide an in-app or website notice.</p>
          <p>Questions can be started through the <a href={withBase("support/")}>NutCracker Tools Support page</a> or the <a href={SITE.issues}>project issue tracker</a>. Do not place sensitive information in a public issue.</p>
          <p><strong>Operator:</strong> {SITE.operator}<br /><strong>Product:</strong> {SITE.name}<br /><strong>Android package:</strong> {SITE.packageName}<br /><strong>Effective date:</strong> {effectiveDate}</p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
